module b3;

<*
 One node of a dynamic tree. Siblings sit as a pair at an even index; the root is node 0 and node 1
 is always empty. Bit 31 of flag_index marks a leaf and bit 30 a node box3d has marked moved; the
 low 30 bits are a leaf's proxy id or an internal node's sibling pair. The union holds an internal
 node's height or a leaf's shape index, the low 32 bits of its proxy's user data.
*>
struct TreeNode {
    Aabb aabb;
    uint flag_index;
    union {
        int height;
        int shape_index;
    }
}

<*
 One proxy of a dynamic tree, at the index its id names. node is the leaf holding its box, or -1
 for a free slot, whose next links the free list.
*>
struct TreeProxy {
    ulong user_data;
    ulong category_bits;
    int node;
    int next;
}

<*
 A bounding volume hierarchy over proxies, held by value: 112 bytes pointing at arrays box3d
 allocated, so a copy leaves two structs sharing them and only one may be destroyed. Pass it on as
 &tree; the arrays move as they grow, so neither nodes nor proxies is a pointer to hold on to. A
 proxy id indexes proxies, not nodes.
*>
struct DynamicTree {
    ulong version;
    TreeNode* nodes;
    int* parents;
    TreeProxy* proxies;
    int node_end;
    int node_capacity;
    int pair_free_list;
    int proxy_count;
    int proxy_capacity;
    int proxy_free_list;
    TreeNode* swap_nodes;
    int* leaf_indices;
    TreeNode* leaf_nodes;
    Aabb* leaf_boxes;
    Vec3* leaf_centers;
    int* bin_indices;
    int rebuild_capacity;
    bool dfs_ordered;
}

<*
 Builds an empty tree and the node and proxy arrays it inserts into.

 proxy_capacity is a starting size and never a ceiling: box3d raises anything below 16 — a negative
 argument included — to 16, so every argument from 16 down builds the same tree, and the arrays grow
 past it as needed. The tree comes back by value and the caller owns it: dynamic_tree_destroy() it
 exactly once, and see DynamicTree for what a copy means. The 112-byte struct is the caller's own
 storage; the arrays are on byte_count(), as a world or a mesh is.
*>
extern fn DynamicTree dynamic_tree_create(int proxy_capacity) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Create");

<*
 Releases the node, parent and proxy arrays and the rebuild arrays, then zeroes the tree, so a
 second call on the same struct frees nothing. That protects no copy: a copy still holds the freed
 pointers. The zero it leaves is not the empty-tree state: height(), every query and creating a
 proxy read through a null array and crash. area_ratio(), root_bounds(), byte_count() and
 dynamic_tree_rebuild() do not, and a save writes nothing.
*>
extern fn void dynamic_tree_destroy(DynamicTree* tree) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Destroy");

<*
 Writes the tree to the path: the struct with its pointers and rebuild scratch zeroed, then the
 nodes and parents up to node_end and the whole proxy array, free slots included. A path box3d
 cannot open, or a destroyed tree, is a silent no-op — it returns nothing a caller could read,
 which is why this takes no fault where load_tree() does.
*>
extern fn void dynamic_tree_save(DynamicTree* tree, ZString path) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Save");

<*
 Raw tree load. Private because load_tree() reads the zeroed tree box3d answers every refusal
 with as the fault it is.
*>
extern fn DynamicTree dynamic_tree_load_raw(ZString path, float scale) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Load") @private;

extern fn int dynamic_tree_create_proxy_raw(
  DynamicTree* tree,
  Aabb aabb,
  ulong category_bits,
  ulong user_data,
) @cname("b3DynamicTree_CreateProxy") @private;

<*
 Raw proxy calls that index the proxy array on an id the caller supplies, under assertions this
 build compiles out. src/tree.c3 carries the guards and is the only public way in.
*>
extern fn void dynamic_tree_destroy_proxy_raw(DynamicTree* tree, int proxy_id) @cname("b3DynamicTree_DestroyProxy") @private;
extern fn void dynamic_tree_move_proxy_raw(DynamicTree* tree, int proxy_id, Aabb aabb) @cname("b3DynamicTree_MoveProxy") @private;
extern fn void dynamic_tree_enlarge_proxy_raw(DynamicTree* tree, int proxy_id, Aabb aabb) @cname("b3DynamicTree_EnlargeProxy") @private;
extern fn void set_category_bits_raw(DynamicTree* tree, int proxy_id, ulong category_bits) @cname("b3DynamicTree_SetCategoryBits") @private;
extern fn ulong category_bits_raw(DynamicTree* tree, int proxy_id) @cname("b3DynamicTree_GetCategoryBits") @private;

<*
 The number of levels below the root, so a tree holding a single proxy reports 0 and so does an
 empty one.
*>
extern fn int DynamicTree.height(&self) @cname("b3DynamicTree_GetHeight");

<*
 The summed perimeter of the internal nodes over the root's. Leaves and the root are skipped, so
 a tree whose only internal node is the root reports 0, as does an empty tree. A root with no
 perimeter, which proxies holding the zero box give at any count, reports 0 too.
*>
extern fn float DynamicTree.area_ratio(&self) @cname("b3DynamicTree_GetAreaRatio");

<*
 The box containing every proxy. An empty tree reports the type's zero rather than an inverted or
 otherwise marked box, so a caller cannot tell it from a degenerate box at the origin.
*>
extern fn Aabb DynamicTree.root_bounds(&self) @cname("b3DynamicTree_GetRootBounds");

<*
 Arithmetic over the capacities rather than a reading of the heap: it counts the caller's own
 112-byte struct, which is not on the heap, and after a rebuild two arrays box3d's median split
 never allocates. The module's byte_count() is the number the allocator agrees with.
*>
extern fn int DynamicTree.byte_count(&self) @cname("b3DynamicTree_GetByteCount");

<*
 Rebalances the tree and returns the number of boxes it sorted. A partial build keeps every
 subtree box3d has not marked moved and so sorts far fewer boxes than a full one — but any
 enlarge_proxy() marks the whole ancestor chain, so one of those undoes the saving. Either build
 allocates a spare node array and the rebuild arrays when it has something to sort, and
 byte_count() carries them.
*>
extern fn int dynamic_tree_rebuild(DynamicTree* tree, bool full_build) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Rebuild");

<*
 Called once for each proxy a query finds. Returning false ends the traversal, and the tree must
 not be modified from inside the call: the traversal carries node indices across it, and a proxy
 created or destroyed there makes them name something else.
*>
alias TreeQueryFn = fn bool(int proxy_id, ulong user_data, void* context);

<*
 Called once for each proxy a closest query reaches, given the search bound as it stands. Only a
 return value below that bound takes effect, so the callback is what measures anything — see
 dynamic_tree_query_closest(). TreeQueryFn's rule against modifying the tree holds here too, and
 for the same reason.
*>
alias TreeClosestFn = fn float(float search_bound, int proxy_id, ulong user_data, void* context);

<*
 Reports proxies whose box overlaps aabb, calling fcn once for each until it returns false — every
 one, unless the tree exhausts box3d's 512-entry traversal stack, which drops a whole subtree and
 reports the short answer as a normal one. Only a degenerate tree of nested boxes reaches that.

 The mask flags do not refine each other: loosely a proxy passes on any bit in common, strictly on
 every bit of the mask, and only a single-bit mask agrees for every proxy. A zero mask reports
 nothing loosely and everything strictly.
*>
extern fn TreeStats dynamic_tree_query(DynamicTree* tree,
  Aabb aabb,
  ulong mask_bits,
  bool require_all_bits,
  TreeQueryFn fcn,
  void* context,
) @cname("b3DynamicTree_Query");

<*
 The closest traversal, whose in-out float box3d calls minDistanceSqr. src/tree.c3 takes that
 bound by value and is the only public way in.
*>
extern fn TreeStats dynamic_tree_query_closest_raw(
  DynamicTree* tree,
  Vec3 point,
  ulong mask_bits,
  bool require_all_bits,
  TreeClosestFn fcn,
  void* context,
  float* search_bound,
) @cname("b3DynamicTree_QueryClosest") @private;

<*
 What a closest query ended with: box3d's traversal counters and the bound the callback left,
 which is the bound the query started with when nothing returned anything smaller. A C3 type with
 no C counterpart — box3d passes the bound back through the in-out float it was given.
*>
struct ClosestSearch {
    TreeStats stats;
    float search_bound;
}

<*
 An axis-aligned box swept along translation, in the tree's own frame. The box never rotates, and
 what travels is its centre: dynamic_tree_box_cast() grows each node box by this one's half-extents
 on all three axes and runs the centre's line through that, so the box enters the sweep only as a
 centre and a size.
*>
struct BoxCastInput {
    Aabb box;
    Vec3 translation;
    float max_fraction;
}

<*
 Called once for each proxy a ray cast reaches, given the input with max_fraction narrowed to the
 bound the cast currently holds. Returning 0 ends the traversal, a value between 0 and that bound
 clips the ray to it, and the bound itself continues unclipped; anything negative does neither,
 which is how box3d skips a proxy. TreeQueryFn's rule against modifying the tree holds here too.
*>
alias TreeRayCastFn = fn float(RayCastInput* input, int proxy_id, ulong user_data, void* context);

<*
 The box cast's counterpart to TreeRayCastFn, over a BoxCastInput and with the same return
 contract and the same rule against modifying the tree.
*>
alias TreeBoxCastFn = fn float(BoxCastInput* input, int proxy_id, ulong user_data, void* context);

<*
 Reports the proxies whose box the ray crosses, calling fcn once for each. This is the broad phase
 alone — a proxy is offered because the ray met its box, and the exact cast against whatever that
 box holds is the callback's work. dynamic_tree_query() gives the mask flags and the traversal
 stack's limit, which apply here too, and TreeRayCastFn gives what fcn's return does.
*>
extern fn TreeStats dynamic_tree_ray_cast(DynamicTree* tree,
  RayCastInput* input,
  ulong mask_bits,
  bool require_all_bits,
  TreeRayCastFn fcn,
  void* context,
) @cname("b3DynamicTree_RayCast");

<*
 Sweeps the input's box along its translation and reports the proxies it reaches, on the terms
 dynamic_tree_ray_cast() states and with TreeBoxCastFn in place of TreeRayCastFn. See BoxCastInput
 for what box3d sweeps, which is not the box itself.
*>
extern fn TreeStats dynamic_tree_box_cast(DynamicTree* tree,
  BoxCastInput* input,
  ulong mask_bits,
  bool require_all_bits,
  TreeBoxCastFn fcn,
  void* context,
) @cname("b3DynamicTree_BoxCast");
